module gunbc.repo_self_build

import std.types { String, List, NonEmptyStr }

// THE SINGLE POINT THROUGH WHICH THIS REPOSITORY BUILDS ITS OWN BINARIES.
// Before this module the same operation was spelled independently in three
// workflow authorities and hand-authored in a fourth, with the bin sets
// differing and nothing relating them, so "how this repo builds its compiler"
// had four owners and could be changed inconsistently -- which it already had
// been. Operator ruling 2026-08-24: everything compiles through the same
// point, no forks allowed.
//
// The split follows DESIGN section 3. The cargo BUILD SHAPE is upstream's and
// belongs to extdeps.rust.cargo. WHICH package and WHICH bins is business
// policy and lives here. How a consumer transports it -- a workflow RunStep, a
// host effect, an interpreter service call -- is realization and lives with
// the consumer. A caller that writes the argv itself has taken all three back.
//
// The construction is not novel: witness_floor_workflow already folded a bin
// roster onto one base command, correctly, in private. What was missing was
// not the idea but its REACH -- three other consumers could not call it. This
// module is that function promoted to where every consumer can reach it.

data repo_self_build_authority_note: String = "Single authority for the argv this repository uses to build its own binaries. The package is a fact about THIS repository's layout, not about cargo, so it is data here rather than a parameter every caller re-supplies: a caller free to name the package could build a different crate and still look like a conforming call. The BIN SET is a parameter because it genuinely differs by consumer -- the required floor needs claim_executor and gunbc, fleet-desired needs gunbc alone -- and that difference is the only axis on which honest callers disagree. THE CARGO ARGV WORDS HERE ARE A REAL FORK AGAINST extdeps.rust.cargo_build AND CONSUMING IT IS NOT AVAILABLE, which review 55636 raised and which is recorded with its measurement so the next author does not attempt the drop-in and discover this alone. Every cargo argv word in that module -- \"cargo\", \"build\", \"-p\", \"--bin\" -- appears ONLY inside a transport shell block: measured on the live file, its entire declaration surface is one ExternalAuthority anchor, one env map, one note and two jobs-flag fns, and no data or fn exposes the vocabulary at all. So there is no expression a fn in this module could read it from, and the argv is fused to the shell transport that spells it. That is exactly the DESIGN section 3 interface-versus-realization seam: this consumer needs a workflow YAML STRING, which is a second realization of the same operation, so the correct repair is to DE-FUSE the operation shape from transport shell and bind both realizations to it -- not to have this module reach into a transport block, which is not a thing the language admits. The follow-up is therefore de-fusion in extdeps, strictly larger than this PR and correctly outside it; until it lands the fork stands, named rather than silent."

data repo_self_build_axes_deferred_note: String = "TWO AXES WERE BUILT HERE AND BACKED OUT BEFORE MERGE, recorded so the next author does not re-derive them from scratch. A CargoBuildSubject coproduct (RepositoryCrate | EmittedCandidateTree { manifest_path }) and a CargoVerb (CargoBuild | CargoCheck) were added for the self-host regen gate, which must cargo-check an EMITTED CANDIDATE TREE -- a different crate, which the package-as-data decision above structurally excludes -- and for which check suffices at a measured 59.8s/2.5GB against build at 155s/4.7GB. Review 55633 called them speculative widening and was right: nothing in that diff consumed either axis, the sole caller pinned both to their defaults, and DESIGN section 6 names a new artifact with no final consumer as experimental residue. The same standard had just been applied one PR over, where an unwired affected-set lens was split out of this branch for exactly this reason; leaving these in would have been that principle applied to one artifact and not its neighbour. THE DESIGN STANDS AND IS NOT RE-OPENED BY THE BACK-OUT: a free package parameter is the wrong remedy because it discards the property the note above relies on, and a sibling authority sharing this argv vocabulary is the section 3 fork this module exists to prevent -- so the subject belongs here as a CLOSED vocabulary, and it lands in the diff that carries its consumer rather than ahead of it."

data repo_self_build_package: NonEmptyStr = "v1-compiler"

data repo_self_build_profile_flag: String = "--release"

data repo_self_build_empty_bins_note: String = "An empty bin set renders the package build with no --bin selector, which is cargo's own meaning for it (build every target in the package) and is a DIFFERENT request from building a named subset -- not a degenerate case of one. It is rendered rather than refused because a fold over an empty roster produces exactly this string in the construction this module promotes, so the state is reachable by an honest caller; a refusal here would break a caller that is asking cargo a legitimate question."

fn repo_self_build_bin_flag(bin: String) -> String {
  join(["--bin ", bin], "")
}

fn repo_self_build_bin_selector(bins: List<String>) -> String {
  join(bins |> map(b => repo_self_build_bin_flag(bin: b)), " ")
}

fn repo_self_build_package_command() -> String {
  join(
    ["cargo build", repo_self_build_profile_flag, "-p", repo_self_build_package as String],
    " "
  )
}

fn repo_self_build_command(bins: List<String>) -> String {
  if bins |> count == 0 {
    repo_self_build_package_command()
  } else {
    join([repo_self_build_package_command(), repo_self_build_bin_selector(bins: bins)], " ")
  }
}
